# Line endings. Without this file, a Windows clone with the default
# `core.autocrlf=true` reports twenty-six files as modified with an EMPTY
# diff — git wants CRLF in the working tree, the files have LF, and the
# content is identical. The visible cost is that `git merge --ff-only`
# refuses to run: "your local changes would be overwritten", about changes
# that do not exist. That happened, and it cost an afternoon.
#
# LF everywhere. This repository is written on Linux and used on Windows;
# Python, Rust, Node and PowerShell all read LF without complaint, and
# Notepad has handled it since 2018.
* text=auto eol=lf

# THE IMPORTANT LINE. `-text` means no conversion in either direction, ever.
#
# A unified diff's context lines have to match the target file byte for byte.
# If git rewrote a patch's endings on checkout, every hunk would carry CRLF
# context against an LF source and `git apply` would refuse the lot — and the
# error it prints ("patch does not apply") says nothing about line endings, so
# the natural conclusion is that the patch is wrong rather than that git
# edited it in transit.
*.patch -text
*.diff -text

# Binary, so git never inspects or converts them.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.wav  binary
*.mp3  binary
*.onnx binary
